[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Polymorphic Data Modeling and Inheritance in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"NoSQL databases like Firebase Firestore are schema-less, which offers incredible flexibility but introduces architectural challenges when dealing with object-oriented concepts like polymorphism and inheritance. In traditional relational databases, inheritance is handled via table-per-class or joined tables. In Firestore, developers must adopt specific document design patterns to represent hierarchical data models efficiently."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Problem"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Imagine an e-commerce platform where you have a base class called 'Product'. You might have specialized subclasses like 'Electronics' and 'Clothing'. Each subclass shares common fields like name, price, and id, but contains unique attributes (e.g., warrantyPeriod for electronics and sizeOrColor for clothing). Storing these in Firestore requires a strategy that allows both uniform querying (all products) and type-specific retrieval."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Strategy 1: Single Collection with Discriminator Field"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The most common and efficient pattern in Firestore is the Single Collection pattern utilizing a discriminator field. All documents live in the same collection (e.g., 'products'), and a specific field like 'type' or 'kind' dictates how the application maps the document to a specific class."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"// Firestore Document: products/prod_123
{
  "id": "prod_123",
  "name": "Wireless Headphones",
  "price": 99.99,
  "type": "ELECTRONICS",
  "warrantyPeriod": "2 Years"
}

// Firestore Document: products/prod_456
{
  "id": "prod_456",
  "name": "Cotton T-Shirt",
  "price": 19.99,
  "type": "CLOTHING",
  "size": "L"
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Polymorphism in Application Code"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"When fetching documents from the 'products' collection, your data access layer inspects the 'type' field to instantiate the correct subclass. Here is a conceptual TypeScript implementation using a factory pattern:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"abstract class Product {
  constructor(public id: string, public name: string, public price: number) {}
}

class ElectronicProduct extends Product {
  constructor(id: string, name: string, price: number, public warrantyPeriod: string) {
    super(id, name, price);
  }
}

class ClothingProduct extends Product {
  constructor(id: string, name: string, price: number, public size: string) {
    super(id, name, price);
  }
}

function productFactory(data: any): Product {
  switch (data.type) {
    case "ELECTRONICS":
      return new ElectronicProduct(data.id, data.name, data.price, data.warrantyPeriod);
    case "CLOTHING":
      return new ClothingProduct(data.id, data.name, data.price, data.size);
    default:
      throw new Error("Unknown product type: " + data.type);
  }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Pros and Cons of the Single Collection Pattern"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pro: Easy to query across all types (e.g., fetching all products under a certain price regardless of category)."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pro: Simplifies pagination and sorting for mixed lists."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Con: Documents may contain sparse fields (null values if not structured carefully), though Firestore handles missing fields gracefully."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Strategy 2: Class-Table / Sub-Collection Inheritance"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"If your subclasses have vastly different schemas and rarely need to be queried together, you can separate them into distinct sub-collections or root collections. However, this breaks polymorphism at the database query level, requiring multiple round-trips if you need an aggregated list."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Polymorphism in Firebase Firestore is best achieved using the Single Collection pattern paired with a discriminator field and a factory method in your application layer. This approach maximizes Firestore's query capabilities while keeping your domain models strictly typed and object-oriented."}]
